CTFontGetUnitsPerEm was a stub returning zero, and zero is a DIVISOR.

Qt scales every glyph advance by pixelSize divided by units per em, so a stub returning 0 made each
advance an infinity. This is the same shape as IOSurfaceAlignProperty in patch 0053 and it is worth
naming as a class: a stub that answers 0 for a size, a stride, an alignment or a divisor is not a
harmless no-op, it is a wrong answer the caller has no way to question.

The font already knows the number. KTFont reads it from its CGFont at init, and KTFont_FT has the
FreeType face behind that, so the answer is one call away. 2048 is the OpenType default and the
only sane fallback.

The stub fired on EVERY key press in CMake, twelve times in one startup, which is how it was found.

HONEST RESULT: this did NOT fix the symptom it was found under. CMake still shows no typed text in
its line edit. Measured after the fix: keys are delivered in 4 runs of 4, the text reaches Qt (the
caret position scales exactly with the character count, about 6.9 pixels per glyph at both 4 and 12
characters), and no glyph is composited for the field. So this is a real defect fixed on its own
terms, not the cause of the empty field. Task #227.

--- a/CoreText/CTFont.m
+++ b/CoreText/CTFont.m
@@ -567,8 +567,16 @@
 
 unsigned int CTFontGetUnitsPerEm(CTFontRef font)
 {
-    printf("STUB %s\n", __PRETTY_FUNCTION__);
-    return 0;
+    /* ZERO IS A DIVISOR HERE, NOT A MISSING VALUE. Qt scales every glyph advance by
+     * pixelSize/unitsPerEm, so the stub made each advance an infinity: a typed character landed
+     * nowhere, the caret never moved off the left edge, and the field looked as though it had
+     * never received the key. The font already knows the number, from its CGFont. 2048 is the
+     * OpenType default and the only sane answer if it does not. Task #227. */
+    if (font == NULL)
+        return 2048;
+
+    unsigned int units = (unsigned int) CGFontGetUnitsPerEm([font graphicsFont]);
+    return units != 0 ? units : 2048;
 }
 
 CFIndex CTFontGetGlyphCount(CTFontRef font) {
